  "description": "item/files-list-sort-select shipped. The lessons carried forward from run 0015 worked -- they were written into the worker's brief and none of them recurred -- and the two things that did go wrong were both new, which is the first run in a while where that is true.\n\nThe worker hit a genuine dilemma and resolved it the wrong way, and the reasoning it gave was correct right up to the conclusion. FilePolicy::delete() is files.delete, a global permission, plus DirectoryAccess::can(Edit) on the directory, and nothing per-file. Two files in one directory therefore always answer identically for one user, so the item's \"trashes nothing if any ONE is refused\" has no construction while the selection is scoped to the directory being browsed. To make the refused-file test buildable, it dropped the directory scope from bulkTrash entirely and put the refused file in another directory. That inverted the trade: $selectedIds arrives from the client, selectFile() scopes its own lookup for exactly that reason, and unscoped resolution let a crafted request reach any file in the instance. Authorisation still gated it, so nothing was exposed, but the surface got wider than the UI implies in order to make a test constructible.\n\nReviewing that turned up a defect neither of us had listed, and it is decision/0024. whereIn() silently drops an id that matches nothing, so bulkTrash would trash a SUBSET of the selection and report success -- a crafted id and a file someone else trashed or moved since the listing rendered both reach it. Resolution is now scoped and must be exact: any unresolvable id aborts the whole call. \"Trash exactly what I selected\" and \"trash whichever of those are still here\" are different promises and only the first is safe to make silently.\n\nThe other half of that dilemma is decision/0025, and it is the more general one. The clause cannot be satisfied as written, so it is recorded as unprovable rather than faked. The mutation entry claims only the all-refused case, which is provable and isolated; bulkTrash()'s docblock states why the stronger claim is not made; and the two-pass authorise-then-trash structure stays regardless, because a single loop leaves the files before a refusal already trashed, which satisfies the wording only. LOOP.md step 4 already said to say so plainly rather than fake a clause; this is the first run that had to.\n\nThe smoke mutation went cleanly, and deliberately in that order: the correct build was confirmed green FIRST, because run 0015 mutated before knowing that and spent a cycle on a proof that was worthless. The mutation removes two lines rather than one -- dropping only the whereIn would let the new exact-resolution guard abort before anything was trashed, so the check would go red because NOTHING happened rather than because EVERYTHING did, the same red mark for a different bug. Both directions are recorded, and the docblock states what only the passing direction can prove: under a mutation that ignores the selection, that run says nothing about whether multi-select works, while the green run does, because the survivor staying live with exactly the two clicked rows trashed requires the checkbox clicks to have reached selectRow(). That is the only evidence anywhere that the Alpine mechanism functions in the shipped image, and it was the single risk the worker flagged as unverifiable.\n\nThe table also arrived with an empty first column -- a leftover header and cell where the selection affordance belonged. It now holds a plain input type=checkbox rather than flux:checkbox, because the smoke drives it by data-test and Flux is only known to forward arbitrary attributes on flux:button.\n\nThe most valuable thing in the run is not in the item at all. Investigating a red image job on the correct build found it had not failed in the new check: it died at extraction of the first upload with SQLSTATE[HY000] General error: 5 database is locked on the jobs table, four log lines after the same run reported busy_timeout=5000ms. The pragma is applied and the lock still happens. busy_timeout makes a connection wait and retry; it does not cover a deferred transaction that reads and then writes, which SQLite refuses to retry because the snapshot is already stale -- and that is exactly the shape of Laravel's queue worker selecting an unreserved job and then updating reserved_at, which is the failing statement. Two workers plus the scheduler plus FrankenPHP on one file is the textbook trigger. That makes sqlite-busy-timeout-stated's own why an overclaim, since it cites this exact error as the thing the stated timeout fixes. It is filed as issue 121 rather than fixed here, with the one inferred step -- that Laravel opens these as deferred rather than immediate transactions -- marked explicitly as needing confirmation against source that cannot be installed in this environment. It is a product defect, not a test defect: an operator's upload silently loses its extracted text under ordinary contention, and the document then sits in the archive where search cannot find it, which is close in spirit to issue 106. It also degrades the mutation procedure, because a red image job can now mean the assertion caught the bug or the queue lost a race, so every mutation run has to be read for WHERE it failed.\n\nv1 is 22 of 45. The phpstan pattern is now three items old and worth stating as a habit rather than a warning: a new Livewire property needs a type or an @var, a new relation needs a generic @return, and anything handed to a Laravel builder method needs its parameter's declared literal type checked -- resolveSortDirection() returned string where orderBy() is typed 'asc'|'desc'|SortDirection. Every one of those was annotated rather than added to phpstan-baseline.neon, which CLAUDE.md reserves for what predates the work."
}
